ata there because you have that  time because your analysis analyzing  your data but when you're communicating  about your data when you have found that  Insight through exploratory data  analysis and you want to tell someone  you want it to be immediately clear from  the visualization that you're presenting  what their conclusion should be or what  they should take from this graph  and it goes beyond that because you  probably don't only want to show that  you did some work with that  visualization you want to start a  discussion or you want to lead into a  certain conclusion  and that's what explanatory data  visualization is about  because explanatory data visualization  tells a story  so maybe your analysis revealed that  recent changes to a marketing campaign  improved the way that people look at  your company and therefore you want to  start a discussion of whether to  continue with that or recommend to  continue with that or maybe you've  discovered that chickens grow the best  on a certain diet and therefore you  recommend continuing with that  the point is an explanatory data  visualization isn't there to be pretty  um it's there to support a point and  while data may be objective the  decisions that you make on how to  present that data also shapes the story  that you're telling  look at this chart for example this is a  chart about the population growth in the  past 60 years and it looks like there's  there's quite a well there's quite an  increase of population growth maybe  looks a bit worrying  but if you look at the other graph which  is from the exact same data set but we  chose the limits of the graph a little  bit different it tells a different story  it tells oh it's getting really critical  here  and I believe that our role as data  storytellers is to bridge the gap  between clinical and analytical data and  tell a story while keeping true to the  facts so how can you make your  visualization in such a way that we  focus on this discussion that we want to  have the point that we want to make  without being misleading  so how can we tell a story with her data  like I said I'm a hobbyist Storyteller I  like writing stories and what we usually  think about when we think about Stories  the three aspects which are not just  beginning with one and are the place or  the context in which the story takes  place the people or the characters in  the story and the purpose or the  motivation of the story itself  and when we think about data  storytelling we don't necessarily of  course have a place or people but we're  also focusing on three things first of  all on the foundation of the  visualization so how do we choose to  represent the data itself and what  decisions do we make there  Focus what kind of characteristics can  we add to our graph or plot to direct  the focus of the reader to the point  where we want their attention  and third forward why am i showing  someone this graph or this plot what  discussion do I want to start or what do  I want them to conclude from this and  that's also what I'll be focusing on in  this presentation  so we'll start with the foundation of  course so this is about how do you  choose the right chart and how do you  choose to represent your data  of course there are lots of different  charts out there there are line plots  Scatter Plots pie charts bar charts lots  of other types of plots as well  and while all of them have some purpose  and are good in certain circumstances  some charts are better in certain  conditions than others  for example if we look at these two  charts they represent the exact same  information but if you had to choose  which one was clearer to get the trend  from is that option A or option D I  would say that option A is the better  choice here because it's it's more  obvious when you're looking at it with  the trend over time is  that doesn't mean that bar charts are  bad it just means that for this for  communicating insights about the trend  maybe the bar chart isn't the right  choice  and the same goes for these two graphs  again they show exactly the same  information  um but if you're interested in seeing  what is the largest uh Mark chair I  think the second one is a little bit  clearer because in the first one the  blocks look roughly the same size when  it's in the second one you can see that  it's just a bit bigger would it even  help to have a little grid on the  background there  so the question is how do you determine  the right turn for your use case well I  would say there are two things you can  do there first of all which I really  recommend anyone just do is  experimentation and experimentation  means that you have a specific thing in  mind that you want to communicate for  instance I want to open a conversation  about the trend and for instance I think  it's going down and you choose a couple  of different charts and see which one  immediately seems the most sensible but  also it doesn't make sense to reinvent  the wheel all the time so something that  I found very helpful as well is this  chart by chartguide.com poster which  tells you about all the different chart  types and which ones are good in certain  in certain circumstances this is  actually kind of a very small version of  it the entire poster is a lot bigger and  I wholeheartedly recommend it  but even if you choose the right chart  there's still a couple of things  regarding how you represent the data  that you also should keep in mind  but first  it's important is to make sure that your  reader or the person who you're  presenting to is familiar with your type  of chart  this might sound very obvious but a  mistake that I very often see happening  is that you know we as data scientists  we like things like confidence intervals  and error labels and those kinds of  things because they give you a lot more  insight into the data they give you some  idea about how precise the management is  they can give you ideas about new things  and new hypothesis you have to try out  but it's probably a little bit too  cluttered and not very clear for the  person who you're presenting to is  usually not necessarily a data scientist  so for example  um box plots takes these Bots plots for  example these are box plots that  represent the attack value for all the  different Pokemon types and they  communicate a lot more they also  communicate not just the mean or the  median but they also something about the  range of the values there  um and there's just a lot going on here  so when I show you this as data  scientists you probably recognize box  plots but box plots are not as common  outside of exploratory data analysis as  you might think so if you have something  like this on a slide you're probably  also going to have to show this in the  slide before where you explain how the  interquartile range is calculated how  that relates to the lower whisker and  the maximum whisper and what kind of  conclusions you can draw from that and  if you have to show this before you show  the box plots you're probably going to  lose your audience there so it's not a  very clear way to clearly communicate  the second point is  choose the data to display carefully  so we already saw that in this example  where the limits and the range of the  axis actually makes a lot of difference  in first impression that you get from  the chart  and this might be obvious but it's  something to really keep in mind and  very often when you make a visualization  even if if it's an easy visualization  you have a lot of choices there that you  have to make for instance these are  again three examples of the exact same  data set where it's about the total  sales of two booksellers  and in the first one even though you can  read the axis and you can draw the  conclusions from that your first  impression is going to be that  Bookseller a is a lot more successful  than Bookseller B whereas if you change  the ranges to start at zero like you  should  um 